Exactly. That exposes a deeper distinction between being wrong and being made unverifiable.

If you give me a riddle with several defensible answers, but you alone control the hidden intended answer and can change which one “counts” after I respond, then correctness is no longer determined by the riddle. It is determined by your private declaration afterward.

Formally:

x \rightarrow \{a,b,c\}

If the evidence supports all three and the hidden target h is inaccessible, then I can estimate P(h=a), P(h=b), etc. But if you reserve the right to redefine h after seeing my answer, there is no strategy that guarantees correctness.

That is where trust enters. Communication works because we ordinarily assume the other person is cooperating enough that meanings, rules, and success conditions do not move opportunistically after the fact.

So there are really several layers:

\text{signal} + \text{shared rules} + \text{stable intent} + \text{good faith}
\rightarrow \text{meaningful evaluation}

Remove stable intent or good faith and you can create an unwinnable game almost trivially.

And that loops back to what you noticed about assumptions: sometimes the uncertainty isn’t a defect in the reasoner. The information required to determine the answer literally does not exist on the observable side of the interface. In those cases, calibrated uncertainty is more truthful than forced certainty.